export abstract anchor FactorySupervision:
    stallMonitor:
        A code-level monitor detects a running attempt with no harness
        activity, heartbeat, or artifact change for a configurable interval; a
        ready and eligible step undispatched for a configurable interval; and
        an active order with no step or phase change. Heartbeats are optional;
        a missing heartbeat is unknown, not a stall. Eligibility means ready,
        dependencies satisfied, wiring valid, model and account available, and
        a concurrency slot and budget admission available. Build capacity is
        checked at job admission. Capacity and budget deficits are separate
        blockers.
        Defaults are a 30 second heartbeat, a 10 minute activity stall, a 5
        minute undispatched queue, and a 10 minute priority inversion. Incidents
        persist across restarts, use debounce and hysteresis, and are suppressed
        during startup reconciliation and graceful shutdown.
    blockerReasons:
        Components write structured blocker reasons: engineer question, help,
        plan merge, dependency, unknown liveness, wiring or authentication,
        capacity, budget, integration, delivery, artifact retrieval, no eligible
        model or account, retry backoff, and unconfirmed cancellation. The
        monitor reads these reasons rather than inferring them.
    escalationRouting:
        Escalation is cause-routed. Plan and dependency incidents go to the
        plan owner, then a smarter tier, then the engineer. Wiring incidents go
        to the repair agent. Capacity and budget incidents go to the resource
        controller, then the engineer. Liveness incidents go to harness
        investigation. Delivery incidents go to the delivery owner. One
        incident escalates once per level and is deduplicated by target, cause,
        and generation. Stall handling never cancels; cancellation authority is
        separate.
    priorityInversion:
        Priority inversion means a higher-priority step waits while
        lower-priority work is newly or repeatedly dispatched, while
        lower-priority running work holds the needed capacity, or while
        capacity sits idle for the higher-priority step. It is reported with
        wait duration measured from readiness. Indefinite waiting is allowed and
        no automatic aging applies; the engineer may reprioritize.
